WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online TOEFL preparation.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.037
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.337 --> 00:00:17.736
Unit fourteen: Writing: Academic Discussion Post. Today you will contribute a clear, supported opinion to an online class discussion.

4
00:00:18.036 --> 00:00:20.723
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.667 --> 00:00:24.725
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.583 --> 00:00:26.956
Discussion board.

7
00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:31.389
Discussion board means an online space for class comments.

8
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:33.716
Post on the discussion board.

9
00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:37.898
Your turn. Say: Discussion board.

10
00:00:40.733 --> 00:00:42.152
Discussion board.

11
00:00:42.917 --> 00:00:43.836
Contribute.

12
00:00:44.850 --> 00:00:47.918
Contribute means to add your ideas to a conversation.

13
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:50.226
Contribute a new idea.

14
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:53.388
Your turn. Say: Contribute.

15
00:00:56.233 --> 00:00:57.316
Contribute.

16
00:00:58.050 --> 00:00:58.923
Position.

17
00:00:59.917 --> 00:01:02.253
Position means your opinion on a question.

18
00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.583
State your position clearly.

19
00:01:05.183 --> 00:01:07.592
Your turn. Say: Position.

20
00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:11.295
Position.

21
00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:12.935
Support.

22
00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.309
Support means to give reasons for an idea.

23
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:18.893
Support your view with an example.

24
00:01:19.483 --> 00:01:21.753
Your turn. Say: Support.

25
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:25.543
Support.

26
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:27.296
Respond.

27
00:01:28.283 --> 00:01:31.119
Respond means to answer another person's idea.

28
00:01:31.717 --> 00:01:33.507
Respond to a classmate.

29
00:01:34.117 --> 00:01:37.048
Your turn. Say: Respond.

30
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:40.959
Respond.

31
00:01:41.717 --> 00:01:42.381
Add.

32
00:01:43.383 --> 00:01:45.487
Add means to bring in a new idea.

33
00:01:46.083 --> 00:01:47.456
Add a different point.

34
00:01:48.050 --> 00:01:50.714
Your turn. Say: Add.

35
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:54.452
Add.

36
00:01:55.217 --> 00:01:56.241
Example.

37
00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.782
Example means a specific case that supports an idea.

38
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:02.710
Give an example.

39
00:02:03.317 --> 00:02:05.946
Your turn. Say: Example.

40
00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:09.906
Example.

41
00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:11.581
Relevant.

42
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:14.838
Relevant means connected to the topic.

43
00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.846
Keep your post relevant.

44
00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.975
Your turn. Say: Relevant.

45
00:02:22.833 --> 00:02:23.834
Relevant.

46
00:02:24.633 --> 00:02:28.203
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:29.050 --> 00:02:32.315
Visal, what is the Academic Discussion task?

48
00:02:33.417 --> 00:02:44.173
You read a professor's question and two students' replies on an online board. Then you write your own post, adding your opinion and reasons, in about ten minutes.

49
00:02:45.283 --> 00:02:47.062
What should my post include?

50
00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:58.302
State your position in the first sentence, give one or two reasons with an example, and refer to a classmate's idea. A short conclusion is optional.

51
00:02:59.417 --> 00:03:00.847
How long should it be?

52
00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:12.010
Aim for about a hundred to a hundred and twenty words. Quality matters more than length, so stay on topic and use clear, accurate sentences.

53
00:03:13.167 --> 00:03:15.062
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:15.917 --> 00:03:19.983
Grammar focus: Strategy: position, reason, example.

55
00:03:20.283 --> 00:03:21.504
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:26.248
I agree with Kim that remote classes are useful, because they save time.

57
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:30.148
For example, I can review the recorded lesson at night.

58
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:39.372
Read the question and both replies. State your position, support it with a reason and an example, and connect your idea to what a classmate said.

59
00:03:40.117 --> 00:03:44.650
Be careful! Don't say: I only copy a classmate's idea.

60
00:03:46.600 --> 00:03:49.610
Say: I give my own reason and example.

61
00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:51.363
Support your position.

62
00:03:52.167 --> 00:03:55.028
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:55.833 --> 00:03:57.659
Online or On Campus.

64
00:03:57.959 --> 00:04:01.735
The professor asks if classes should be online or on campus.

65
00:04:02.733 --> 00:04:05.534
One student says online classes save time.

66
00:04:06.533 --> 00:04:09.566
Another says on-campus classes help friendships.

67
00:04:10.567 --> 00:04:13.774
Maly agrees with the first student and adds a new reason.

68
00:04:14.767 --> 00:04:18.473
She writes that recorded lessons help her review before exams.

69
00:04:19.567 --> 00:04:23.801
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.671
An Academic Discussion post should start with blank.

71
00:04:27.971 --> 00:04:31.985
A, a goodbye. B, a list of numbers. C, your position.

72
00:04:35.533 --> 00:04:37.904
The answer is C: your position.

73
00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:40.098
You should blank.

74
00:04:40.398 --> 00:04:45.630
A, support your idea. B, copy a classmate. C, write nothing.

75
00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.561
The answer is A: support your idea.

76
00:04:52.717 --> 00:04:54.496
About how long is a good post?

77
00:04:54.796 --> 00:05:00.701
A, 1000 words. B, 100 to 120 words. C, 10 words.

78
00:05:04.267 --> 00:05:08.066
The answer is B: 100 to 120 words.

79
00:05:09.217 --> 00:05:11.170
What new reason does Maly add?

80
00:05:11.470 --> 00:05:17.074
A, classes are free. B, teachers are kind. C, recorded lessons help review.

81
00:05:20.633 --> 00:05:23.376
The answer is C: recorded lessons help review.

82
00:05:24.517 --> 00:05:26.040
The post must be blank.

83
00:05:26.340 --> 00:05:30.818
A, relevant. B, off-topic. C, a poem.

84
00:05:34.367 --> 00:05:36.227
The answer is A: relevant.

85
00:05:37.433 --> 00:05:40.747
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:41.583 --> 00:05:44.105
How do you write an academic discussion post?

87
00:05:52.917 --> 00:05:54.382
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:54.682 --> 00:06:08.668
I read the professor's question and the two student replies. Then I state my position and give a reason with an example. I also mention a classmate's idea so that my post joins the discussion.

89
00:06:08.968 --> 00:06:10.655
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:17.627
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: position, reason, example.

91
00:06:17.927 --> 00:06:22.739
Today's value is respectful disagreement. Disagree politely and listen to others.

92
00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:25.842
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

93
00:06:26.142 --> 00:06:29.387
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

